worst-case guarantee, not a typical one.

\section{The boundary: fixed steps can fail forever}\label{sec:cycle}

\begin{theorem}[Exact two-cycle]\label{thm:cycle}
Take samples $y=(0,1,2)$, so $N=3$ and the unique minimizer is $\theta^\star=1$. Fixed-step
subgradient descent with $\eta=3$ from $\theta_0=3$ produces $\theta_n=3$ for $n$ even and
$\theta_n=-6$ for $n$ odd.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction. At $\theta=3$ all three samples satisfy $y_i\le3$, so $g(3)=3$ and the next iterate
is $3-3\cdot3=-6$. At $\theta=-6$ all three samples exceed $-6$, so $g(-6)=-3$ and the next
iterate is $-6+9=3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$|\theta_n-\theta^\star|\ge2$ for every $n$.
\end{corollary}

Thus the literal statement ``gradient descent on a tropical loss converges to a tropical
rational minimizer'' is \emph{false} for a fixed step. The mechanism is purely tropical:
outside the convex hull of the data the subgradient is constant of magnitude $N$, so the
iterate translates by the fixed amount $\eta N$ however far away it is. There is no restoring
force proportional to the error --- exactly what curvature would have supplied. The correct
statements are the best-iterate bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:sqrt} with a $1/\sqrt n$ schedule,
and the self-tuning Polyak bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:troppolyak}. Sharpness bounds the loss
from below but says nothing about the step rule: a method that ignores the loss value cannot
use it.

\section{Comparison with rectifier networks}\label{sec:relu}

\begin{theorem}[Risk-landscape equivalence]\label{thm:landscape}
For $f:\R\to\R$ the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is tropical rational;
\item there is a rectifier expression $e$ with $\mathcal R_N(f;X,Y)=\mathcal R_N(e;X,Y)$ for
every $N$ and all data $X,Y$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(1)\Rightarrow(2)$: Theorem~\ref{thm:compile} produces $e$ with $e=f$ pointwise, and equal
functions have equal risks. $(2)\Rightarrow(1)$: fix $x$ and apply the hypothesis to the
one-point data set $N=1$, $X_0=x$, $Y_0=f(x)$. The left side is $0$, so $|e(x)-f(x)|=0$; as
$x$ was arbitrary, $e=f$ pointwise and $f$ is tropical rational by Theorem~\ref{thm:fwd}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The tropical and rectifier hypothesis classes have identical loss landscapes: the same
minimizers, sharpness constants and Lipschitz constants, hence exactly the same first-order
convergence rates. Every rate of Sections~\ref{sec:fixed}--\ref{sec:polyak} transfers verbatim.
\end{corollary}

One might hope that the observed speed of training a rectifier network reflects something
about the architecture. Theorem~\ref{thm:landscape} says that, at the level of what a
first-order method sees, the architecture contributes nothing the tropical description does
not already contain. The rate is determined by $(\mu,G)$, both of which are read off from the
sample geometry and the monomial slopes.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Tropical evaluation]
Evaluate $P_{b,\ell}(x)$ by a single fold: $\Theta(k)$ time, $\Theta(1)$ space, $k=|\ell|+1$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Tropical-to-rectifier compilation]
Convert $P_{b,\ell}$ by the recursion of Construction~\ref{con:compile}: $\Theta(k)$ nodes,
$k-1$ rectifiers, depth $\Theta(k)$, or $\Theta(\log k)$ with a balanced binary-tree variant of
Lemma~\ref{lem:max}.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Tropical product]
Given polynomials with $k$ and $k'$ monomials, form the pairwise-sum polynomial with $kk'$
monomials by Lemma~\ref{lem:prod} in $\Theta(kk')$ time; prune monomials that never attain the
maximum by an upper-hull sweep in $\Theta(kk'\log(kk'))$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Polyak-step tropical training]
Repeat $\theta\leftarrow\theta-\frac{R(\theta)-R^\star}{g(\theta)^2}g(\theta)$ until
$g(\theta)=0$. Each iteration costs $\Theta(N)$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:troppolyak} the iteration
count to accuracy $\varepsilon$ is $O(N^2\log(D/\varepsilon))$, total
$O(N^3\log(D/\varepsilon))$. (Sorting solves the one-dimensional median problem exactly in
$\Theta(N\log N)$; the iterative method is of interest as the analysable prototype of the
multivariate case, where no closed form exists.)
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Dequantization schedule]
Train the smooth unit at temperatures $T_j\downarrow0$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:neuron} the
objective at temperature $T$ is within $NT\log k$ of the tropical objective uniformly, so
$T=\varepsilon/(2N\log k)$ makes the smooth surrogate's optimum $\varepsilon$-optimal for the
tropical problem.
\end{algorithmbox}

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Slope and sharpness replace smoothness and strong convexity.}
Every rate proved here is controlled by two numbers: $G$, the maximal tropical slope, which is
simultaneously the Lipschitz constant of the loss and the subgradient bound; and $\mu$, the
sharpness constant, the linear growth rate away from the optimum. Their ratio
$\mu/G\in(0,1]$ plays the role of an inverse condition number: smooth strongly convex gradient
descent contracts by $1-m/L$ per step; sharp Lipschitz Polyak descent contracts squared
distance by $1-\mu^2/G^2$. In the tropical setting both constants are combinatorial.

\paragraph{Why the loss stays tropical.}
The model is tropical, the loss is tropical (Proposition~\ref{prop:losstrop}), and every level
set and subdifferential is polyhedral: optimization never leaves the tropical category. A
squared-error loss on a tropical model is piecewise quadratic --- smoother, but it destroys the
exact combinatorial description and with it the sharpness constant.

\paragraph{Dequantization as a physical limit.}
The bound $\max\le\LSE_T\le\max+T\log k$ is the learning-theoretic form of the zero-temperature
limit: $\LSE_T$ is $-T$ times a free energy, its $T\to0$ limit a ground-state energy, and the
$T\log k$ error an entropy term bounded by the logarithm of the number of states. Uniformity
in the input and logarithmic dependence on width make the tropical model a legitimate proxy.

\paragraph{Limits of the present analysis.}
The results are one-dimensional in parameter and input. The sharpness proof uses the reflection
pairing and the betweenness lemma, both one-dimensional; in $\R^d$ the $L^1$ minimizer is a
coordinatewise median only for separable losses. The identification of $G$ with the largest
slope generalizes (as the largest dual norm of a monomial gradient), but identifying $\mu$
requires knowing which normal cone of the tropical hypersurface contains the optimum. The
product rule generalizes verbatim, so the algebra is not the difficulty; the geometry of the
optimum is.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[A tropical condition number governs all first-order rates]
For a tropical rational loss $L$ on $\R^d$, with $G$ its maximal tropical slope and $\mu$ its
sharpness constant at the minimizer, Polyak-step subgradient descent contracts the squared
distance by exactly $1-\mu^2/G^2$ per step, and no first-order method beats
$(1-\mu^2/G^2)^{n/2}$ on the worst instance with these invariants.
\end{conjecture}

The point is that $\mu/G$ is purely combinatorial --- the ratio between the smallest and largest
slopes of the tropical normal fan at the optimum --- so the optimization rate is computed from
the geometry of a Newton polytope rather than from any analytic estimate. The one-variable case
is settled here ($\mu=1$, $G=N$, contraction $1-1/N^2$), and the multivariate normal-fan
machinery needed for the matching lower bound is elementary polyhedral combinatorics.

\begin{conjecture}[Dequantization commutes with training]
Let $\theta_T(n)$ be the $n$-th gradient-descent iterate of the smooth exp--log network at
temperature $T$, and $\theta_0(n)$ the $n$-th subgradient iterate of its tropical limit. Then
$|\theta_T(n)-\theta_0(n)|\le C\,n\,T\log k$, and consequently the two training trajectories
have the same limit set as $T\to0^{+}$.
\end{conjecture}

The reason to expect this is that the dequantization defect is uniformly $T\log k$ per unit
(Theorem~\ref{thm:neuron}), so it can accumulate at most linearly along a trajectory of
$1$-Lipschitz update maps; the missing ingredient is a discrete Gr\"onwall argument for
nonexpansive piecewise-linear update maps.

\paragraph{Further problems.}
(i) Multivariate tropical rational calculus: the pairwise-sum product rule generalizes, but a
substitute for the median/betweenness argument is needed. (ii) Pruning: characterize
inessential tropical monomials (those never attaining the maximum), giving a canonical minimal
rectifier realization and exact tropical model compression. (iii) Stochastic and mini-batch
Polyak steps where $R^\star$ must be estimated online. (iv) Generalization: sharpness at the
empirical optimum plus a uniform slope bound should yield fast excess-risk rates, since sharp
empirical risk pins down the parameter rather than merely the loss value. (v) Depth: the
compilation of Construction~\ref{con:compile} has depth linear in the monomial count;
quantifying the depth/monomial trade-off would give a tropical account of the expressivity
benefits of depth.

\section{Conclusion}

Freezing an exp--log network --- sending its weight scale to infinity, or its temperature to
zero --- costs at most $T\log k$ uniformly and turns it into a max-plus object. In one variable
that object is exactly a rectifier network, monomial for monomial. Its absolute-error training
landscape is a convex piecewise-linear function that is sharp with an explicit constant, and
sharpness, not smoothness, determines how fast first-order methods run. With a fixed step the
method may cycle forever; with a $1/\sqrt n$ schedule it achieves the optimal general rate;
with the self-tuning Polyak step it converges geometrically at rate $1-\mu^2/G^2$, which for
the tropical median problem is $1-1/N^2$. Since a rectifier network computing the same function
has an identical risk landscape, the speed is a property of the tropical geometry of the loss
and not of the parameterization. Two combinatorial invariants --- the largest slope and the
growth constant --- determine everything.

